# SYNOPSIS

```usage
man [-tw] [section ...] title [node]
lookman key ...
sig name ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`man` prints the page of the manual that documents <title>, from `/lib/man`, refilled to 80 columns and indented as Plan 9's pages are.
It looks in each <section> given, in order, or in sections 1 to 8, and prints the first page it finds.
A page is found by any name it documents, so `man lookman` prints this page.
With a <node>, it prints that node of the page alone.
NAME is made from the page's header and SOURCE from its sources.
There is no pager: the terminal scrolls.

: `-t`
  Print the page's contents instead: its headings, and each node's id and title with the node's headings under it.
: `-w`
  Print the page's path instead.

`lookman` prints a line for each page whose names, summary or keys hold every <key> as a word, in any case: the `man` command that shows the page, and its summary.

`sig` prints the declarations of each <name> from the SYNOPSIS of the section 2 page that documents it, for pasting into code.
A name such as `Handle.close` is found by its page and printed by its last part.

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% man 6 guide fences
% man -w manifest
/lib/man/6/manifest
% lookman manifest
man 6 manifest # the launcher's service manifests
```

# FILES

: `/lib/man/<section>/<page>`
  The pages, in guide(6).
: `/lib/man/index`
  The index: a row for each name a page documents and for each node, a tab between its fields: the name, the page, its section, the node, the summary and the page's keys.

# SEE ALSO

guide(6), intro(1), vx-man(1) on the dev machine

# DIAGNOSTICS

`man` exits with `no manual page for <title>` when no page documents it, `lookman` with `no match` when no page matches, and `sig` with `no declaration` when a name has none.

